b. Divide both parts of $12:30$ by $3$ to produce another equivalent ratio.

examples.solution_text:
a. Splitting every cell into two equal cells doubles both counts.
[tikz]% mathsmap-diagram-colours {"version":1,"kind":"geometry","base":[],"semantic":[{"name":"templateShade","hex":"D4E8FF","reason":"Each original cell is split into two, doubling both counts to four shaded cells and ten unshaded cells."}],"reference":"template equivalent-ratios-doubled"}
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1mm,y=1mm]
\special{dvisvgm:raw <metadata data-graph-strokes="1"/>}
\definecolor{templateShade}{HTML}{D4E8FF}
\path[use as bounding box] (-0.6,-12.6) rectangle (35.6,0.6);
\fill[templateShade] (0,-6) rectangle (5,0);
\fill[templateShade] (5,-6) rectangle (10,0);
\fill[templateShade] (0,-12) rectangle (5,-6);
\fill[templateShade] (5,-12) rectangle (10,-6);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (5,0)--(5,-12);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (10,0)--(10,-12);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (15,0)--(15,-12);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (20,0)--(20,-12);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (25,0)--(25,-12);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (30,0)--(30,-12);
\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (0,-6)--(35,-6);
\draw[black,line width=0.8pt] (0,0) rectangle (35,-12);
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]
$\begin{aligned}&\mathrel{\phantom{=}}2:5\\&=(2\times2):(5\times2)\\&=4:10\end{aligned}$
b. $\begin{aligned}&\mathrel{\phantom{=}}12:30\\&=(12\div3):(30\div3)\\&=4:10\end{aligned}$
